                        "cot": "\n\nFirst, we need to understand the effects of morphine. Morphine is an opioid analgesic that can cause side effects such as itching, urinary retention, and respiratory depression. It can also cause nausea and vomiting, but this is less common.\n\nIn this case, the patient already had vomiting and retching due to biliary duct stricture. The patient was given 3mg of morphine epidurally daily, which means that the patient was already receiving morphine and had not shown any adverse effects.\n\nHowever, one day, the patient was mistakenly given a 12mg dose of epidural morphine. This is a significantly higher dose than the patient was receiving daily. The question is, what are the possible effects of this higher dose?\n\nOption A: Itching - Itching is a common side effect of morphine, but it is not relevant to this case. The question is asking about the effects of the higher dose of morphine, not the regular dose.\n\nOption B: Urinary retention - Urinary retention is another common side effect of morphine, but it is also not relevant to this case. The question is asking about the effects of the higher dose of morphine, not the regular dose.\n\nOption C: Increase vomiting - Vomiting is a possible side effect of morphine, but it is less common than nausea. In this case, the patient was already experiencing vomiting and retching due to biliary duct stricture. It is possible that the higher dose of morphine could worsen the vomiting, but it is not a definite outcome.\n\nOption D: Overstimulation of respiratory centre - This is the most concerning possible effect of the higher dose of morphine. Morphine can cause respiratory depression, which means that it can slow down breathing. In extreme cases, this can lead to respiratory arrest, which is life-threatening. The higher dose of morphine could increase the risk of respiratory depression and arrest.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D) Overstimulation of respiratory centre.",
