                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe question is asking which of the following symptoms was NOT present in a patient with biliary duct stricture who was given 3mg of morphine epidurally daily, but was mistakenly given a 12mg dose of epidural morphine on one day.\n\nFirst, let's understand what biliary duct stricture is. It is a condition where the bile ducts, which carry bile from the liver to the small intestine, become narrowed or blocked. This can cause symptoms such as abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and jaundice.\n\nMorphine is a strong painkiller that is often used to manage severe pain. It works by binding to opioid receptors in the brain and spinal cord, which reduces the perception of pain.\n\nWhen morphine is given epidurally, it is injected into the space around the spinal cord. This allows it to act directly on the nerves that transmit pain signals, providing more effective pain relief than oral or intravenous administration.\n\nHowever, epidural morphine can also cause side effects, such as itching, urinary retention, nausea, and respiratory depression.\n\nGiven this background, let's consider each of the answer choices:\n\nA) Itching: Itching is a common side effect of morphine, especially when given epidurally. It occurs because morphine can release histamine, which causes itching and other allergic symptoms. Therefore, it is likely that the patient experienced itching as a result of the morphine.\n\nB) Urinary retention: Urinary retention is another common side effect of morphine, especially in older adults or those with pre-existing urinary problems. It occurs because morphine can relax the muscles that control the bladder, making it difficult to urinate. Therefore, it is possible that the patient experienced urinary retention as a result of the morphine.\n\nC) Increase vomiting: Vomiting is a common symptom of biliary duct stricture, and it can be exacerbated by opioids such as morphine. Therefore, it is possible that the patient experienced an increase in vomiting as a result of the morphine.\n\nD) Overstimulation of respiratory centre: Overstimulation of the respiratory center is a rare but serious side effect of morphine. It occurs when the drug suppresses the normal breathing reflex, leading to respiratory depression or even respiratory arrest. However, this is unlikely to have occurred in this case, as the patient was only given a single high dose of morphine epidurally, and not a continuous infusion. Moreover, the patient did not exhibit any signs of respiratory distress or failure.\n\nTherefore, the correct",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "a763323c-54c4-4f5e-a845-9cc4c9436699",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "D) Overstimulation of respiratory centre, as this symptom was not present in the patient.",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
