                        "cot": "\n\nTo determine which natural disaster causes the maximum deaths, we need to consider the following:\n\n1. Hydrological disasters: These include floods, tsunamis, and landslides caused by heavy rainfall. While these disasters can cause significant damage to property and infrastructure, they may not always result in a high number of deaths, especially if people are evacuated in time.\n\n2. Meteorological disasters: These include hurricanes, tornadoes, and cyclones, which can cause widespread destruction and loss of life. However, these disasters are often localized and may not affect a large population.\n\n3. Geological disasters: These include earthquakes, volcanic eruptions, and avalanches, which can cause massive destruction and loss of life. Earthquakes, in particular, can be devastating, as they can trigger tsunamis and landslides.\n\n4. Fires: While not typically considered a natural disaster, wildfires can be caused by natural factors such as lightning strikes or human activity. These fires can spread quickly and cause significant damage to property and loss of life.\n\nBased on the above considerations, it is difficult to determine which natural disaster causes the maximum deaths. However, geological disasters such as earthquakes and volcanic eruptions are known to have caused some of the deadliest natural disasters in history, such as the 2004 Indian Ocean earthquake and tsunami, which claimed over 230,000 lives. Therefore, option C) Geological is the most likely answer.",
