                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient is presenting with altered sensorium, which means there is a change in their mental status. This could be due to a variety of reasons, including infections, metabolic disturbances, or neurological conditions. \n\nOption A, Complete blood counts, can help identify any infections or blood-related conditions that could be causing the altered sensorium. Therefore, it should be done.\n\nOption B, Random blood sugar, can help identify if the patient has high or low blood sugar levels, which can cause altered sensorium. Therefore, it should be done.\n\nOption C, Lumbar puncture, is a procedure where a needle is inserted into the spinal canal to collect cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) for testing. This test is usually done to diagnose infections or neurological conditions that could be causing the altered sensorium. Therefore, it should be done.\n\nOption D, Non-contrast CT head, is a type of imaging test that can help identify any structural abnormalities or bleeding in the brain that could be causing the altered sensorium. Therefore, it should also be done.\n\nTherefore, the answer is none of the above. All of these tests should be done to help diagnose the cause of the patient's altered sensorium.",
