                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nAltered sensorium can have various causes, including infections, metabolic disturbances, and neurological disorders. Therefore, it is important to perform a thorough evaluation to identify the underlying cause. \n\nA complete blood count (CBC) and random blood sugar (RBS) are basic tests that can help identify infections and metabolic disturbances, respectively. Therefore, these tests should be done in a patient with altered sensorium. \n\nA lumbar puncture (LP) is a diagnostic test that involves the removal of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) from the spinal canal. It is usually done to evaluate for infections or bleeding in the brain or spinal cord. Therefore, an LP may be indicated in a patient with altered sensorium if there is suspicion of meningitis or encephalitis. \n\nA non-contrast CT head is a radiological test that can help identify structural abnormalities in the brain, such as bleeding, tumors, or swelling. Therefore, a non-contrast CT head may be indicated in a patient with altered sensorium if there is suspicion of a structural abnormality. \n\nBased on the above discussion, it is clear that all of the tests mentioned (CBC, RBS, LP, and non-contrast CT head) may be indicated in a patient with altered sensorium, depending on the suspected underlying cause. Therefore, the correct answer to the question is \"None of the above.\"",
