                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "First, let's understand what each of these options means:\n\nA) Maternal alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) is a protein produced by the fetus and is present in the mother's blood. It is used as a screening test for certain birth defects, but it is not specifically used for assessing the risk of Down's syndrome.\n\nB) Maternal human chorionic gonadotropin (HCG) is a hormone produced by the placenta during pregnancy. It is also used as a screening test for certain birth defects, but it is not specifically used for assessing the risk of Down's syndrome.\n\nC) Ultrasound (USG) is a non-invasive imaging technique that can be used to visualize the fetus during pregnancy. It can be used to assess the risk of Down's syndrome by measuring the nuchal translucency (the fluid-filled space at the back of the fetus's neck) during the first trimester. However, this is a screening test and not a diagnostic test.\n\nD) Chorionic villus sampling (CVS) is an invasive diagnostic test that involves taking a small sample of cells from the placenta. These cells can be analyzed for chromosomal abnormalities, including Down's syndrome. This test can provide a definitive diagnosis of Down's syndrome.\n\nGiven these options, the best choice for assessing the risk of Down's syndrome in the present pregnancy would be:\n\nD) Chorionic villous biopsy",
