                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nWhen we talk about lateral movement of the condyle, we are referring to the movement of the mandible to the side. This movement is achieved by the contraction of the lateral pterygoid muscle, which is located on both sides of the jaw joint. \n\nNow, the lateral pterygoid muscle on the same side as the movement (ipsilateral) contracts to move the mandible to the opposite side. Therefore, option B is correct. \n\nThe contralateral lateral pterygoid muscle also plays a role in lateral movement, but it works to stabilize the opposite side of the jaw joint during the movement. So, option A is not correct. \n\nThe medial pterygoid muscle is involved in elevating the mandible and moving it forward, but it does not contribute to lateral movement. Therefore, option C is not correct. \n\nSo, the correct answer is B) Ipsilateral lateral pterygoid.",
                        "answers": [
