                        "cot_trigger": "zhou-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nFirst, let's define what we mean by \"lateral movement of condyle.\" This refers to the movement of the mandibular condyle (the rounded end of the lower jaw bone) as it slides forward and sideways out of the temporomandibular joint (TMJ) socket.\n\nNext, let's consider the muscles involved in this movement. The lateral pterygoid muscle is the primary muscle responsible for lateral movement of the mandible. However, it is important to note that there are two lateral pterygoid muscles - one on each side of the jaw. Therefore, we need to specify whether we are talking about the contralateral (opposite) or ipsilateral (same) side lateral pterygoid.\n\nThe medial pterygoid muscle is not directly involved in lateral movement of the mandible, but rather in elevating and protracting (moving forward) the mandible.\n\nBased on this information, we can eliminate options C and D as incorrect.\n\nSo, which is it - contralateral or ipsilateral lateral pterygoid? The answer is A) contralateral lateral pterygoid. This muscle contracts to pull the mandible sideways and forward, causing the condyle to move out of the TMJ socket on the opposite side. The ipsilateral lateral pterygoid also contributes to lateral movement, but to a lesser extent.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "90484499-c634-4e3a-8dc5-84449d132ddb",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "A) Contralateral lateral pterygoid.",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
