                    {
                        "id": "86110c9b-ca95-40da-9c07-83c2f6a9183e",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-03",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "it is important to understand what is meant by \"lateral movement of condyle.\" This refers to the movement of the mandibular condyle (the rounded end of the lower jaw bone) as it slides forward and sideways out of the temporomandibular joint (TMJ) socket. This movement is necessary for actions such as opening the mouth wide or moving the jaw from side to side.\n\nWith that in mind, the correct answer is A) Contralateral lateral pterygoid. The lateral pterygoid muscle is responsible for initiating the movement of the mandibular condyle out of the TMJ socket. When the muscle on one side contracts, it pulls the condyle forward and to the opposite side. Therefore, the contralateral (opposite side) lateral pterygoid is responsible for lateral movement of the condyle. The ipsilateral (same side) lateral pterygoid also plays a role in this movement, but it primarily helps to stabilize the condyle during lateral movements. The medial pterygoid muscle is involved in elevating the mandible (closing the mouth) and moving it forward.",
                        "answers": [
