                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nD) Integration of viral DNA with host DNA. \n\nAsymptomatic hepatitis B infection occurs when the virus is present in the body but does not cause any symptoms or liver damage. However, chronic infection with hepatitis B virus (HBV) can lead to the development of hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC), a type of liver cancer. This is because HBV can integrate its DNA into the host cell's DNA, leading to genetic mutations and instability that can promote the development of cancer. Additionally, chronic inflammation caused by the immune response to the virus can also contribute to the development of HCC.",
