                    {
                        "id": "27d4101c-301f-4098-bb8a-3b6a8fc1b923",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": "zhou-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the key features of the case.\n\n- Chronic liver failure and muscle weakness at 3 months of age\n- Low serum glucose, ketoacidosis, and decreased pH\n- Raised ALT and AST\n- Normal blood lactate and uric acid levels\n- Glucagon raises blood glucose levels after meals but not after an overnight fast\n- Increased glycogen in liver on biopsy\n\nStep 2: Understand the role of enzymes in glycogen metabolism.\n\nGlycogen is a complex carbohydrate that is stored in the liver and muscles. It is broken down into glucose when the body needs energy. There are several enzymes involved in glycogen metabolism, including:\n\n- Glycogen synthase: adds glucose molecules to glycogen chains\n- Glycogen phosphorylase: breaks down glycogen into glucose-1-phosphate\n- Debranching enzyme: removes branches from glycogen chains\n- Branching enzyme: adds branches to glycogen chains\n- Glucose-6-phosphatase: converts glucose-6-phosphate to glucose, which can be released into the bloodstream\n\nStep 3: Consider the symptoms and test results in the case.\n\nThe child in this case has low serum glucose, ketoacidosis, and decreased pH, which suggests a problem with glucose metabolism. Glucagon raises blood glucose levels after meals but not after an overnight fast, which suggests a defect in gluconeogenesis (the process by which the body makes glucose from non-carbohydrate sources). The liver biopsy shows increased glycogen, which suggests a problem with glycogen metabolism.\n\nStep 4: Eliminate unlikely options.\n\nOption C (branching enzyme) and option B (muscle phosphorylase) are unlikely to be the cause of the child's symptoms, as they are primarily involved in glycogen synthesis and breakdown in muscle tissue, respectively. The child's symptoms and liver biopsy suggest a problem with glycogen metabolism in the liver.\n\nStep 5: Choose the best answer.\n\nOption A (glucose-6-phosphatase) is the most likely cause of the child's symptoms. Glucose-6-phosphatase is responsible for converting glucose-6-phosphate to glucose, which can be released into the bloodstream. A defect in this enzyme would result in low serum glucose levels and an inability to raise blood glucose levels after an overnight fast. Glucagon can still raise blood glucose levels after meals because it stimulates glycogen breakdown in the liver, which does not require glucose-6-phosphatase.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "b264d89c-dc6f-4642-a3f4-0f658b2040af",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "A) Glucose-6-phosphatase.",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
                        ],
