                    {
                        "id": "017ed340-210a-4097-91d7-18c5dc2d942e",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-01",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe symptoms and investigation findings suggest a glycogen storage disease. The increased glycogen in the liver and low serum glucose levels indicate impaired glycogen breakdown or release of glucose from glycogen. The fact that intravenous glucagon raises blood glucose levels after meals but not after an overnight fast suggests impaired gluconeogenesis, which is the process of producing glucose from non-carbohydrate sources.\n\nOption A, glucose-6-phosphatase, is involved in the final step of gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis, which is the release of glucose from glucose-6-phosphate. Deficiency of this enzyme leads to glycogen storage disease type I (von Gierke disease), which presents with severe hypoglycemia, lactic acidosis, and hepatomegaly. However, the normal blood lactate levels and absence of hepatomegaly in this case make this option less likely.\n\nOption B, muscle phosphorylase, is involved in glycogen breakdown in muscle tissue. Deficiency of this enzyme leads to glycogen storage disease type V (McArdle disease), which presents with exercise intolerance, muscle cramps, and myoglobinuria. However, the muscle weakness in this case is not specific to exercise and the liver biopsy findings suggest a liver-specific glycogen storage disease.\n\nOption C, branching enzyme, is involved in glycogen synthesis and branching. Deficiency of this enzyme leads to glycogen storage disease type IV (Andersen disease), which presents with hepatosplenomegaly, liver cirrhosis, and early death. However, the normal liver size and absence of cirrhosis in this case make this option less likely.\n\nOption D, debranching enzyme, is involved in glycogen breakdown and conversion of glycogen to glucose. Deficiency of this enzyme leads to glycogen storage disease type III (Cori disease), which presents with hepatomegaly, hypoglycemia, and muscle weakness. The normal blood lactate levels and absence of hepatomegaly in this case suggest a milder form of the disease. The fact that intravenous glucagon raises blood glucose levels after meals but not after an overnight fast is consistent with impaired gluconeogenesis, which is a feature of Cori disease.\n\nTherefore, the most likely enzyme to be defective in this child is debranching enzyme (option D).",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "ce78117c-92de-4887-bd26-59f104974c9b",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "D) Debranching enzyme.",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
                        "date": "2023/03/14 16:06:10",
                        "api_service": "openai_chat",
                        "model": "{'name': 'gpt-3.5-turbo', 'temperature': 0, 'max_tokens': 512}",
                        "comment": "",
