                    },
                    {
                        "id": "f8705364-20be-442e-b4b7-2fa01c16fffd",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-03",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "we can eliminate options B, C, and D as they are all involved in glycogen breakdown, whereas this child has increased glycogen in the liver. The enzyme likely to be defective in this child is therefore A) Glucose-6-phosphatase, which is responsible for converting glucose-6-phosphate to glucose, allowing glucose to be released into the bloodstream. Without this enzyme, glucose cannot be released from glycogen stores in the liver, leading to low blood glucose levels and the need for glucagon to raise blood glucose levels after meals. This condition is known as glycogen storage disease type I (also called von Gierke disease).",
                        "answers": [
