                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": " Intraoral radiographs are taken inside the mouth, so the patient is very close to the radiation source. Therefore, it is important to reduce the amount of radiation the patient is exposed to. \n\nA) High speed film - High speed film does not reduce patient radiation. It is used to reduce the amount of time the patient needs to be exposed to radiation.\n\nB) Intensifying screens - Intensifying screens do reduce patient radiation. They are placed between the radiation source and the film, and they absorb some of the radiation before it reaches the film.\n\nC) Lead apron - Lead aprons also reduce patient radiation. They are worn by the patient and absorb some of the radiation before it reaches the patient.\n\nD) Decreasing kVp - Decreasing kVp does reduce patient radiation. kVp stands for kilovolt peak, and it is a measure of the amount of radiation being used. Lowering the kVp will reduce the amount of radiation the patient is exposed to.\n\nThe correct answer is A) High speed film.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "30bd0ade-0d2d-44a8-b90a-b730877965fa",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
