                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe main crystalline component of calculus can be determined through the following steps:\n\n1. What is calculus? \n\nCalculus, also known as tartar, is a hard, mineralized deposit that forms on teeth and can lead to dental problems such as gum disease and tooth decay.\n\n2. What are the components of calculus? \n\nCalculus is primarily composed of mineralized plaque, which is made up of various minerals and organic materials.\n\n3. Which mineral is the main crystalline component of calculus? \n\nResearch has shown that the main mineral component of calculus is hydroxyapatite, which is a form of calcium phosphate. Hydroxyapatite makes up approximately 50-70% of the mineralized plaque in calculus.\n\n4. What about the other options? \n\nOctacalcium phosphate, magnesium whitlockite, and brushite are also minerals that can be found in calculus, but they are not the main crystalline component. Octacalcium phosphate and brushite are actually precursors to hydroxyapatite, meaning they can eventually convert into hydroxyapatite over time.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is A) Hydroxyapatite.",
