                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nGrey communicans are small nerve branches that connect spinal nerves to sympathetic ganglia. They are called \"grey\" because they are composed of unmyelinated nerve fibers, which give them a greyish appearance. Here are the statements and their accuracy:\n\nA) Unmyelinated - True\nB) Connects to spinal nerves - True\nC) Preganglionic - True\nD) Present medial to the white ramus communicans - False\n\nThe correct answer is D) Present medial to the white ramus communicans. Grey communicans are located lateral to the white ramus communicans, not medial. The white ramus communicans is a myelinated nerve fiber that carries preganglionic sympathetic fibers from the spinal cord to the sympathetic ganglia. The grey communicans, on the other hand, carry postganglionic sympathetic fibers from the ganglia to the spinal nerves.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
