                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe stage of the lesion can be determined by using the TNM staging system for cancer. \n\nT - describes the size and extent of the primary tumor\nN - describes the spread to nearby lymph nodes\nM - describes the presence of distant metastasis\n\nHowever, since the given information does not include any details about lymph node involvement or distant metastasis, we can only use the T stage to determine the stage of the lesion.\n\nAccording to the American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) staging system for nasopharyngeal cancer, T1 tumors are confined to the nasopharynx, while T2 tumors extend to the oropharynx or nasal cavity. T3 tumors invade the bony structures of the skull base or paranasal sinuses, and T4 tumors involve the cranial nerves, hypopharynx, or orbit.\n\nBased on the given information, the enhancing mass is seen in the nasopharynx with minimal extension into the sphenoid sinus and no lateral extension. This suggests that the tumor is confined to the nasopharynx and has not invaded any nearby structures. Therefore, the stage of this lesion is T1.\n\nAccording to the AJCC staging system, T1 tumors are classified as stage IA. Therefore, the answer is A) IA.",
