                        "cot_trigger": "zhou-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "First, let's review the staging system for nasopharyngeal carcinoma, which is based on the American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) TNM staging system. The stages are as follows:\n\nStage I: T1, N0, M0\nStage II: \n  - IIA: T1, N1, M0 or T2a, N0, M0\n  - IIB: T2a, N1, M0 or T2b, N0-1, M0\nStage III: T1-2, N2, M0 or T3, N0-2, M0\nStage IV: \n  - IVA: T4, N0-2, M0 or T1-4, N3, M0\n  - IVB: Any T, any N, M1\n\nNow let's break down the information given in the question:\n\n1. The patient is a 16-year-old male.\n2. He has nasal obstruction and recurrent epistaxis for several months.\n3. The CT scan shows an enhancing mass in the nasopharynx with minimal extension into the sphenoid sinus and no lateral extension.\n\nBased on this information, we can determine the T, N, and M classifications:\n\nT: The tumor is in the nasopharynx with minimal extension into the sphenoid sinus and no lateral extension. This would be classified as T1 (tumor confined to the nasopharynx) or T2a (tumor extends to oropharynx and/or nasal cavity without parapharyngeal extension).\n\nN: There is no mention of lymph node involvement in the question, so we can assume N0 (no regional lymph node metastasis).\n\nM: There is no mention of distant metastasis in the question, so we can assume M0 (no distant metastasis).\n\nBased on these classifications, the lesion would be staged as IIA (T1, N1, M0 or T2a, N0, M0). Therefore, the correct answer is C) IIA.",
                        "answers": [
