                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe correct answer is D) Hypodiploidy.\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n\n- ALL stands for Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia, which is a type of cancer that affects the white blood cells.\n- Prognostic indicators are factors that help predict the outcome or course of a disease. In the case of ALL, several factors can affect the prognosis, such as age, sex, leukocyte count, genetic abnormalities, and response to treatment.\n- Option A) Female sex is not a poor prognostic indicator of ALL. In fact, some studies have shown that females have a better prognosis than males, although the reasons for this are not entirely clear.\n- Option B) Leukocyte count < 50,000 is not a poor prognostic indicator either. In general, patients with lower leukocyte counts tend to have a better prognosis than those with higher counts, but this is not always the case.\n- Option C) Age greater than 1 year is also not a poor prognostic indicator per se. However, age can affect the prognosis in different ways depending on the patient's age group. For example, infants with ALL have a worse prognosis than older children or adults.\n- Option D) Hypodiploidy is a poor prognostic indicator of ALL. Hypodiploidy refers to a genetic abnormality in which the cancer cells have fewer than the normal number of chromosomes. This abnormality is associated with a higher risk of treatment failure and relapse, and patients with hypodiploid ALL generally have a worse prognosis than those without it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D) Hypodiploidy.",
                        "answers": [
