                        "cot": "Hepatic first-pass metabolism refers to the process where a drug is metabolized in the liver before it reaches systemic circulation. Bypassing this process means that the drug will enter the bloodstream without being metabolized by the liver first.\n\nA) Oral: Drugs administered orally are absorbed through the gastrointestinal tract and then transported to the liver via the portal vein. This means that they undergo hepatic first-pass metabolism. So, this route does not bypass the process.\n\nB) Intravenous: Drugs administered intravenously are directly injected into the bloodstream, bypassing the liver and avoiding hepatic first-pass metabolism.\n\nC) Sublingual: Drugs administered sublingually are absorbed through the mucous membranes under the tongue and enter the bloodstream directly, bypassing the liver and avoiding hepatic first-pass metabolism.\n\nD) Intradermal: Drugs administered intradermally are injected into the skin, where they are absorbed into the bloodstream. This route also bypasses the liver and avoids hepatic first-pass metabolism.\n\nBased on this information, the correct answer is:\n\nA) Oral",
