                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the key information in the scenario.\n- 40 years old male\n- History of fall\n- One episode of vomiting near an alcohol shop\n- Awake with open eyes\n- Retrograde amnesia\n\nStep 2: Consider the possible causes based on the information given.\n- Diffuse axonal injury: This is a type of brain injury that occurs when the brain is shaken or rotated inside the skull, causing damage to the axons (nerve fibers) that connect brain cells. It can result from a severe blow to the head, such as a car accident or a fall from a height. Symptoms may include loss of consciousness, coma, or persistent vegetative state. However, the scenario does not mention any loss of consciousness or coma, so this is less likely to be the cause.\n- Concussion: This is a type of mild traumatic brain injury that occurs when the brain is jolted or shaken inside the skull, causing temporary disruption of brain function. It can result from a blow to the head, such as a fall or a sports injury. Symptoms may include headache, dizziness, confusion, or memory loss. This seems like a possible cause based on the scenario.\n- Drunkenness: This refers to the state of being intoxicated with alcohol, which can impair cognitive function and coordination. It can result from excessive drinking or alcohol abuse. However, the scenario does not provide enough information to support this as the cause.\n- Cerebral venous thrombosis: This is a rare type of stroke that occurs when a blood clot forms in the veins that drain blood from the brain, causing a backup of blood and increased pressure inside the skull. It can result from various factors, such as dehydration, infection, or genetic disorders. Symptoms may include headache, seizures, or focal neurological deficits. This is less likely to be the cause based on the scenario.\n\nStep 3: Choose the most likely cause based on the information given.\nBased on the information given, the most likely cause of the patient's symptoms is concussion. This is because he had a fall and retrograde amnesia, which are common symptoms of concussion. The episode of vomiting near an alcohol shop may or may not be related to the fall, but it does not necessarily indicate drunkenness as the cause. Diffuse axonal injury and cerebral venous thrombosis are less likely based on the absence of certain symptoms in the scenario.",
                        "answers": [
