                    {
                        "id": "8287e2a6-ed36-49fc-ae75-19e3231501cc",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-01",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe Krebs cycle, also known as the citric acid cycle, is a series of chemical reactions that occur in the mitochondria of cells. It is an important metabolic pathway that generates energy by breaking down carbohydrates, fats, and proteins. Acetyl-CoA is a key molecule that enters the Krebs cycle and is formed from the breakdown of pyruvate, fatty acids, and some amino acids. \n\nTo determine which of the given amino acids does not enter the Krebs cycle by forming Acetyl-CoA pyruvate, we need to examine the metabolic pathways of each amino acid. \n\nA) Glycine: Glycine is a non-essential amino acid that can be synthesized from serine. It can be converted to pyruvate through a series of reactions that involve the glycine cleavage system. Pyruvate can then enter the Krebs cycle by forming Acetyl-CoA. Therefore, glycine can enter the Krebs cycle by forming Acetyl-CoA pyruvate. \n\nB) Tyrosine: Tyrosine is a non-essential amino acid that can be synthesized from phenylalanine. It can be converted to fumarate through a series of reactions that involve the tyrosine catabolic pathway. Fumarate can then enter the Krebs cycle by forming malate. Therefore, tyrosine can indirectly enter the Krebs cycle, but not by forming Acetyl-CoA pyruvate. \n\nC) Hydroxyproline: Hydroxyproline is a non-essential amino acid that is derived from proline. It is not directly involved in the Krebs cycle and does not enter it by forming Acetyl-CoA pyruvate. \n\nD) Alanine: Alanine is a non-essential amino acid that can be synthesized from pyruvate. It can be converted back to pyruvate through a series of reactions that involve the alanine cycle. Pyruvate can then enter the Krebs cycle by forming Acetyl-CoA. Therefore, alanine can enter the Krebs cycle by forming Acetyl-CoA pyruvate. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C) Hydroxyproline.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "8e2b6ebe-2717-4f1e-9165-509a85ce8156",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "C) Hydroxyproline.",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
                        ],
