                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nNet filtration pressure (NFP) is the difference between the forces that promote filtration and those that oppose it. It is calculated as follows:\n\nNFP = (capillary hydrostatic pressure + interstitial oncotic pressure) - (capillary oncotic pressure + interstitial hydrostatic pressure)\n\nGiven that NFP should be 3 mm Hg, and the values of interstitial hydrostatic pressure, interstitial oncotic pressure, and capillary hydrostatic pressure are 2 mm Hg, 7 mm Hg, and 25 mm Hg, respectively, we can rearrange the equation to solve for capillary oncotic pressure:\n\ncapillary oncotic pressure = (capillary hydrostatic pressure + interstitial oncotic pressure) - (interstitial hydrostatic pressure + NFP)\n\ncapillary oncotic pressure = (25 mm Hg + 7 mm Hg) - (2 mm Hg + 3 mm Hg)\n\ncapillary oncotic pressure = 29 mm Hg - 5 mm Hg\n\ncapillary oncotic pressure = 24 mm Hg\n\nTherefore, the capillary oncotic pressure required to allow a net filtration pressure of 3 mm Hg is 24 mm Hg. The closest option is C) 23, but the correct answer is actually not listed.",
