                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe reverse bevel incision is a type of incision used in periodontal surgery. It involves making an incision on the gingiva (gum tissue) around a tooth, with the bevel facing towards the tooth. This creates a flap of tissue that can be lifted to access the underlying structures.\n\nA) Allow atraumatic reflection of the gingival margin: This is true. The reverse bevel incision is designed to minimize trauma to the gingival margin (the edge of the gum tissue) when the flap is reflected (lifted). By making the incision with the bevel facing towards the tooth, the tissue is cut at an angle that allows it to be lifted more easily and with less trauma.\n\nB) Remove the infected tissue in the sulcus: This is partially true. The reverse bevel incision can be used to access and remove infected tissue in the sulcus (the space between the tooth and the gum tissue). However, this is not the primary purpose of the incision.\n\nC) Provide access to the alveolar crest: This is also true. The reverse bevel incision can be used to access the alveolar crest (the bony ridge that supports the teeth) in order to perform procedures such as osseous surgery (reshaping the bone) or bone grafting.\n\nD) All of the above: This is the correct answer. The reverse bevel incision is used to allow atraumatic reflection of the gingival margin, remove infected tissue in the sulcus, and provide access to the alveolar crest.",
