                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-09",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe reverse bevel incision is a type of incision used in periodontal surgery. It involves making an incision on the gingiva (gum tissue) in a way that the bevel (angled edge) of the incision faces towards the tooth, rather than away from it. This creates a flap of tissue that can be lifted to access the underlying structures.\n\nNow, let's consider the options:\n\nA) Allow atraumatic reflection of the gingival margin.\nThis statement is true. The reverse bevel incision is designed to minimize trauma to the gingival margin (the edge of the gum tissue) when the flap is reflected (lifted). By angling the incision towards the tooth, the flap can be lifted without damaging the delicate tissue at the margin.\n\nB) Remove the infected tissue in the sulcus.\nThis statement is partially true. The reverse bevel incision can be used to access the sulcus (the space between the tooth and the gum tissue) and remove infected tissue. However, this is not the primary purpose of the incision.\n\nC) Provide access to the alveolar crest.\nThis statement is also partially true. The reverse bevel incision can be used to access the alveolar crest (the bony ridge that supports the teeth), but again, this is not the primary purpose of the incision.\n\nD) All of the above\nBased on the above analysis, we can see that option D is the most accurate answer. The reverse bevel incision is designed to allow atraumatic reflection of the gingival margin, provide access to the sulcus and alveolar crest, and remove infected tissue as needed.",
                        "answers": [
