                        "cot": "A) A reverse bevel incision is indeed made to allow atraumatic reflection of the gingival margin. This type of incision helps to preserve the gingival tissue and minimize trauma during the procedure.\n\nB) The reverse bevel incision can also be used to remove infected tissue in the sulcus. By making this incision, the dentist or periodontist can access and remove the diseased tissue without causing unnecessary damage to the surrounding healthy tissue.\n\nC) Providing access to the alveolar crest is another purpose of the reverse bevel incision. This access allows the dentist or periodontist to perform procedures such as bone grafting or periodontal regeneration.\n\nConsidering all these points, the correct answer is:\n\nD) All of the above",
