                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe given scenario describes a 24-year-old college student who injured his right knee while playing hockey. After 3 months, the patient presents with instability of the knee joint in full extension but without instability at 90 degrees of flexion. \n\nBased on this information, we can narrow down the possible structures that may have been damaged during the initial injury. The anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) and posterior cruciate ligament (PCL) are two major ligaments that provide stability to the knee joint. The medial meniscus is a cartilage structure that also helps in stabilizing the knee joint. \n\nSince the patient is experiencing instability in full extension, it is likely that the ACL has been damaged. The ACL is responsible for preventing anterior translation of the tibia on the femur, and instability in full extension is a common sign of ACL injury. \n\nOut of the given options, the most likely structure that has been damaged is the anteromedial portion of the ACL (option B). The posterolateral portion of the ACL (option A) is less commonly injured, and the PCL (option C) is not likely to cause instability in full extension. The anterior horn of the medial meniscus (option D) may cause locking or catching sensations in the knee joint, but it is not likely to cause instability in full extension. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) Anteromedial portion of the anterior cruciate ligament.",
                        "answers": [
