                        "cot": "\n\nThe fact that the patient has instability in full extension but not at 90 degrees of flexion suggests that the injury is likely to be to a structure that is more taut in extension than in flexion. The anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) fits this description, as it is more taut in extension and helps to prevent anterior translation of the tibia on the femur. \n\nOf the options given, the most likely answer is therefore A) Posterolateral part of the anterior cruciate ligament. This area of the ACL is particularly vulnerable to injury, as it is the last part of the ligament to become taut during knee extension and is therefore more likely to be damaged in hyperextension injuries. \n\nB) Anteromedial part of the ACL is less commonly injured, and C) the posterior cruciate ligament and D) the anterior horn of the medial meniscus would not typically cause instability in full extension without also causing instability at 90 degrees of flexion.",
