                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step 1: Identify the symptoms\n- Instability of the knee joint in full extension\n- No instability at 90 degrees of flexion\n\nStep 2: Analyze the symptoms\n- The instability in full extension suggests that there is damage to a structure that provides stability when the knee is straightened.\n- The lack of instability at 90 degrees of flexion suggests that the damaged structure is not involved in stabilizing the knee when it is bent.\n\nStep 3: Match the symptoms to the structures\nA) Posterolateral part of the anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) - The ACL provides stability to the knee joint, but it is not specifically involved in stabilizing the knee in full extension or at 90 degrees of flexion.\nB) Anteromedial part of the anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) - The ACL provides stability to the knee joint, but it is not specifically involved in stabilizing the knee in full extension or at 90 degrees of flexion.\nC) Posterior cruciate ligament (PCL) - The PCL provides stability to the knee joint, particularly in preventing posterior displacement of the tibia on the femur. It is more involved in stabilizing the knee in flexion, so it is less likely to be the damaged structure in this case.\nD) Anterior horn of the medial meniscus - The medial meniscus provides stability to the knee joint, particularly in full extension. Damage to the anterior horn of the medial meniscus could cause instability in full extension without affecting stability at 90 degrees of flexion.\n\nBased on the analysis, the most likely damaged structure in this case is:\n\nD) Anterior horn of the medial meniscus",
