                    {
                        "id": "56f7e621-9371-4f20-bb8d-2fb3046fbf76",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": "zhou-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Understand the condition\nMeniere's disease is a disorder of the inner ear that causes episodes of vertigo, hearing loss, tinnitus, and a feeling of fullness or pressure in the ear. It is caused by an excess buildup of fluid in the inner ear.\n\nStep 2: Understand the options\nThere are several surgical options available to treat Meniere's disease, including:\n\n- Surgical Labyrinthectomy: This involves removing the entire inner ear, which eliminates the source of vertigo but also causes complete hearing loss in the affected ear.\n- Vestibular Neurectomy: This involves cutting the vestibular nerve, which carries balance signals from the inner ear to the brain. This eliminates vertigo but can also cause hearing loss.\n- Endolymphatic Sac Decompression: This involves opening the endolymphatic sac, which is a small pouch in the inner ear that regulates fluid balance. This can reduce the frequency and severity of vertigo attacks.\n- Cochleosacculotomy: This involves making a small hole in the sacculus, which is a part of the inner ear that is involved in balance and hearing. This can reduce vertigo symptoms without affecting hearing.\n\nStep 3: Determine the best option\nThe question asks for the best surgery to relieve intractable vertigo in a Meniere's disease patient. Intractable means that the vertigo is difficult to control or treat with medication or other non-surgical options.\n\nBased on this information, the best option would be Vestibular Neurectomy (B). This surgery eliminates vertigo without affecting hearing, which is important for many patients. However, it should be noted that all of the surgical options have potential risks and benefits, and the best choice will depend on the individual patient's specific situation and preferences.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "995660b0-9af7-4812-a6b5-88edb0422df7",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "B) Vestibular neurectomy.",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
                        ],
