                        "cot": "\n\nFirst, we need to understand what the fetal adrenal gland is and what hormones it produces. The fetal adrenal gland is a gland located on top of the kidneys in a developing fetus. It produces several hormones, including cortisol, aldosterone, and androgens (such as testosterone).\n\nNext, we need to determine which hormone is predominantly released by the fetal adrenal gland. The question gives us four options: estrogen, testosterone, aldosterone, and coisone. We can eliminate estrogen and testosterone because they are not predominantly produced by the fetal adrenal gland. Estrogen is mainly produced by the ovaries in females and testosterone is mainly produced by the testes in males.\n\nThat leaves us with aldosterone and coisone. While both hormones are produced by the fetal adrenal gland, aldosterone is the predominant hormone released. Aldosterone is a steroid hormone that helps regulate blood pressure and electrolyte balance in the body.\n\nTherefore, the answer is C) Aldosterone.",
