                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe given information suggests that the male child has the following symptoms:\n\n1. Coarse facial features\n2. Macroglossia (enlarged tongue)\n3. Thick lips\n4. Copious mucous discharge from nose\n5. Enlarged liver and spleen\n\nThe child was normal at birth, which rules out any congenital abnormalities. The symptoms suggest a metabolic disorder or a genetic syndrome.\n\nHurler's syndrome is a genetic disorder that affects the metabolism of mucopolysaccharides. It presents with similar symptoms as the child, including coarse facial features, macroglossia, and organ enlargement. The copious mucous discharge from the nose is not a typical symptom of Hurler's syndrome, but it can be present due to chronic respiratory infections. Therefore, option A is a possible diagnosis.\n\nBeckwith-Weidman syndrome is a genetic disorder that affects growth and development. It presents with symptoms such as macroglossia, abdominal wall defects, and organ enlargement. However, thick lips and copious mucous discharge from the nose are not typical symptoms of this syndrome. Therefore, option B is unlikely.\n\nHypothyroidism is a condition where the thyroid gland does not produce enough thyroid hormone. It can cause symptoms such as coarse facial features, macroglossia, and organ enlargement. However, thick lips and copious mucous discharge from the nose are not typical symptoms of hypothyroidism. Therefore, option C is unlikely.\n\nProteus syndrome is a rare genetic disorder that causes overgrowth of tissues and organs. It can cause symptoms such as macroglossia and organ enlargement. However, thick lips and copious mucous discharge from the nose are not typical symptoms of this syndrome. Therefore, option D is unlikely.\n\nBased on the given information, the most likely diagnosis is Hurler's syndrome (option A). However, further diagnostic tests such as enzyme assays and genetic testing may be required to confirm the diagnosis.",
