                        "cot_trigger": "zhou-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Understand the question and the terms used\nThe question is asking about a specific type of antibody called anti-centromere antibodies. We need to identify the condition(s) in which these antibodies are seen.\n\nStep 2: Recall the conditions associated with anti-centromere antibodies\nAnti-centromere antibodies are most commonly associated with scleroderma, a connective tissue disorder that affects the skin, blood vessels, and internal organs. These antibodies are found in up to 60% of patients with limited cutaneous scleroderma and in up to 20% of patients with diffuse cutaneous scleroderma.\n\nStep 3: Eliminate the incorrect options\nA) Drug-induced lupus: Anti-centromere antibodies are not typically associated with drug-induced lupus. Instead, drug-induced lupus is characterized by the presence of anti-histone antibodies.\nB) SLE: Anti-centromere antibodies are not typically associated with SLE. Instead, SLE is characterized by the presence of a variety of autoantibodies, including anti-dsDNA, anti-Sm, and anti-Ro/La.\nC) Sjogren syndrome: Anti-centromere antibodies are not typically associated with Sjogren syndrome. Instead, Sjogren syndrome is characterized by the presence of anti-SSA/Ro and anti-SSB/La antibodies.\n\nStep 4: Select the correct option\nD) Scleroderma: Anti-centromere antibodies are most commonly associated with scleroderma, as discussed in Step 2. Therefore, the correct answer is D.\n\nAnswer: D) Scleroderma",
