                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe correct answer is D) Scleroderma.\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n\n- Anti-centromere antibodies are autoantibodies that target the centromere, a region of the chromosome that plays a crucial role in cell division and chromosome segregation.\n- These antibodies are associated with several autoimmune diseases, including Sjogren syndrome, systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE), and scleroderma.\n- Drug-induced lupus is a type of lupus that is caused by certain medications, such as hydralazine and procainamide. It is not typically associated with anti-centromere antibodies.\n- SLE is a systemic autoimmune disease that can affect multiple organs and tissues. While anti-centromere antibodies can be present in some patients with SLE, they are not a defining feature of the disease.\n- Sjogren syndrome is an autoimmune disorder that primarily affects the exocrine glands, leading to dry eyes and mouth. While anti-centromere antibodies can be present in some patients with Sjogren syndrome, they are not a defining feature of the disease.\n- Scleroderma, also known as systemic sclerosis, is a connective tissue disorder that can affect the skin, blood vessels, and internal organs. Anti-centromere antibodies are present in up to 60% of patients with limited cutaneous scleroderma, a subtype of the disease that primarily affects the skin of the hands, face, and feet.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D) Scleroderma.",
