                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-09",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nSIADH (syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion) is characterized by excessive secretion of vasopressin, leading to water retention and dilutional hyponatremia. Pressor escape occurs when the body's compensatory mechanisms overcome the effects of vasopressin and allow for increased urine output and normalization of serum sodium levels.\n\nOption B is the correct answer as it describes the mechanism of pressor escape as a sudden increase in urine volume with a decrease in urine osmolality dependent on circulating vasopressin levels. This occurs when the body's compensatory mechanisms, such as increased atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) and decreased renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS) activity, counteract the effects of vasopressin and promote diuresis.",
