                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nAutoinfection refers to the process by which an individual becomes infected with a parasite by their own fecal-oral route. This can occur when eggs or larvae of the parasite are ingested and then hatch or develop into adult worms within the same host. \n\nA) Enterobius vermicularis, also known as pinworm, is a common cause of autoinfection. The female pinworm lays eggs around the anus, which can be transferred to the mouth through scratching or poor hygiene. \n\nB) Hymenolepis nana, also known as dwarf tapeworm, can also cause autoinfection. The eggs of this tapeworm can hatch within the intestine and infect the host again. \n\nC) Taenia solium, also known as pork tapeworm, can cause autoinfection in rare cases. The larvae of this tapeworm can migrate to other tissues in the body and develop into cysts, which can then release more larvae into the intestine. \n\nD) Ascaris lumbricoides, also known as roundworm, is not typically associated with autoinfection. The eggs of this worm are passed in the feces and must be ingested by a new host to complete the life cycle. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D) Ascaris lumbricoides.",
                        "answers": [
