                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-03",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "let's define autoinfection. Autoinfection is the process by which an individual becomes infected with a parasite from their own body, rather than from an external source. This can occur when the parasite's eggs or larvae are already present in the individual's body and are able to reinfect them. \n\nBased on this definition, we can eliminate options C and D, as Taenia solium and Ascaris lumbricoides are known to be capable of autoinfection. \n\nOption A, Enterobius vermicularis (also known as pinworm), is a common cause of autoinfection. The female pinworm lays eggs around the anus, which can cause itching. When the individual scratches the area, they can transfer the eggs to their fingers and then potentially ingest them, leading to autoinfection. \n\nOption B, Hymenolepis nana (also known as dwarf tapeworm), is also capable of autoinfection. The eggs of this tapeworm can hatch within the intestine and the resulting larvae can reinfect the individual. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) Hymenolepis nana.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "dc656c85-7912-40d9-9f4c-1109aa25da13",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "B) Hymenolepis nana.",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
                        ],
