                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nChicken pox infection in a pregnant mother can be dangerous for the baby because the virus can cross the placenta and infect the developing fetus. The severity of the infection depends on the timing of the infection during pregnancy.\n\nOption A) 14-28 weeks: This is the second trimester of pregnancy. If the mother gets infected with chicken pox during this time, there is a risk of fetal abnormalities, such as limb deformities, eye and brain damage, and growth retardation.\n\nOption B) 3-9 weeks: This is the first trimester of pregnancy, which is the most critical period for fetal development. If the mother gets infected with chicken pox during this time, there is a high risk of miscarriage, stillbirth, or severe fetal abnormalities.\n\nOption C) 28-37 weeks: This is the third trimester of pregnancy. If the mother gets infected with chicken pox during this time, there is a risk of premature delivery, pneumonia, and other complications in the newborn.\n\nOption D) 8-14 weeks: This is also the first trimester of pregnancy, which is the most critical period for fetal development. If the mother gets infected with chicken pox during this time, there is a high risk of miscarriage, stillbirth, or severe fetal abnormalities.\n\nTherefore, the most dangerous time for a mother to get infected with chicken pox during pregnancy is option B) 3-9 weeks.",
