                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe translocation t(ll;22) is a characteristic genetic abnormality found in Ewing's sarcoma. Therefore, detecting this translocation is important for confirming the diagnosis of Ewing's sarcoma. \n\nOption A, conventional karyotyping, is a technique that can be used to visualize chromosomes and detect large-scale chromosomal abnormalities. However, translocation t(ll;22) is a small-scale genetic abnormality that may not be detected by conventional karyotyping. Therefore, option A is not the best method to detect this translocation.\n\nOption B, next-generation sequencing, is a powerful technique that can sequence millions of DNA fragments in parallel. However, this technique may not be necessary to detect a specific translocation like t(ll;22), which involves only two chromosomes. Therefore, option B may not be the best method to detect this translocation.\n\nOption C, FISH (fluorescence in situ hybridization), is a technique that uses fluorescent probes to detect specific DNA sequences in cells. FISH can be used to detect translocations, deletions, and amplifications of specific genes or chromosomal regions. Therefore, option C is a good method to detect translocation t(ll;22) in Ewing's sarcoma.\n\nOption D, PCR (polymerase chain reaction), is a technique that can amplify specific DNA sequences in cells. PCR can be used to detect mutations, deletions, and translocations in specific genes or chromosomal regions. However, PCR may not be the best method to detect translocation t(ll;22) in Ewing's sarcoma, as this translocation involves two different chromosomes. Therefore, option D is not the best method to detect this translocation.\n\nIn conclusion, the best method to detect translocation t(ll;22) in Ewing's sarcoma is option C, FISH.",
