                        "cot": "\n\nThe correct answer is C) Squatting.\n\nTetralogy of Fallot is a congenital heart defect that affects the flow of blood through the heart. It is characterized by four abnormalities in the heart's structure, including a ventricular septal defect, pulmonary stenosis, an overriding aorta, and right ventricular hypertrophy.\n\nIn order to compensate for the reduced blood flow to the lungs, children with tetralogy of Fallot often assume a squatting position. This position helps to increase the blood flow to the lungs by reducing the resistance to blood flow in the pulmonary arteries.\n\nSupine position (lying on the back) and prone position (lying on the stomach) do not provide any benefit in improving blood flow to the lungs. Leaning forward may help to relieve some of the symptoms associated with tetralogy of Fallot, but it is not as effective as squatting.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C) Squatting.",
