                        "cot": "Tetralogy of Fallot is a congenital heart defect that affects the flow of blood through the heart. It consists of four defects: ventricular septal defect, pulmonary stenosis, overriding aorta, and right ventricular hypertrophy. Children with this condition may experience episodes of cyanosis (bluish discoloration of the skin) and shortness of breath, especially during physical activity.\n\nWhen a child with Tetralogy of Fallot experiences these symptoms, they may instinctively adopt a position that helps to increase blood flow to the lungs and reduce the severity of the symptoms. This position is known as the \"Tet spell\" position or \"knee-chest\" position.\n\nThe correct answer is:\n\nC) Squatting\n\nSquatting is the position that a child with Tetralogy of Fallot may use to help alleviate their symptoms during a Tet spell. This position helps to increase the blood flow to the lungs by increasing the pressure in the systemic circulation, which in turn forces more blood through the pulmonary circulation.",
