                        "cot": "A) Seasonal: This refers to a pattern that repeats within a year, typically related to changes in weather or other seasonal factors. This doesn't seem to fit the description of a gradual increase over multiple years.\n\nB) Cyclical: This refers to a pattern that repeats over a longer period, such as economic cycles or other multi-year trends. While this might involve increases and decreases, it doesn't necessarily imply a gradual increase over time.\n\nC) Periodical: This refers to something that occurs at regular intervals, such as a monthly magazine or an annual event. This doesn't necessarily imply a gradual increase over time.\n\nD) Secular: This refers to a long-term, non-repeating trend or change. In this case, a gradual increase in non-communicable disease cases over multiple years would be considered a secular trend.\n\nAnswer: D) Secular",
