                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: What are antigen-presenting cells?\nAntigen-presenting cells (APCs) are a type of immune cell that present antigens (foreign substances) to other immune cells, such as T cells, to initiate an immune response.\n\nStep 2: Which of the options are antigen-presenting cells?\nA) M-cells: Yes, M-cells are a type of APC found in the gut-associated lymphoid tissue.\nB) Macrophages: Yes, macrophages are a type of APC found in various tissues throughout the body.\nC) Langerhans cells: Yes, Langerhans cells are a type of APC found in the skin.\nD) Thymocytes: No, thymocytes are immature T cells that are not APCs.\n\nStep 3: Which option is the correct answer?\nThe correct answer is D) Thymocytes, as they are not antigen-presenting cells.",
