                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Understand the concept of antigen-presenting cells\nAntigen-presenting cells (APCs) are specialized cells that present antigens to T cells, which then activate the immune response. APCs include dendritic cells, macrophages, and B cells.\n\nStep 2: Analyze the options\nA) M-cells: M-cells are specialized cells found in the mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT) that transport antigens from the lumen of the gut to underlying lymphoid tissue. M-cells are not considered APCs.\nB) Macrophages: Macrophages are a type of APC that engulf and digest foreign substances, including pathogens and dead cells.\nC) Langerhans cells: Langerhans cells are a type of dendritic cell found in the skin and mucous membranes. They are considered APCs.\nD) Thymocytes: Thymocytes are immature T cells that develop in the thymus. They are not considered APCs.\n\nStep 3: Determine the correct answer\nBased on the analysis, the correct answer is D) Thymocytes. Thymocytes are not considered APCs, while the other options (M-cells, macrophages, and Langerhans cells) are all types of APCs.",
