                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "A) M-cells: These are specialized epithelial cells found in the mucosal tissues, such as the intestine, and are involved in the uptake and transport of antigens from the lumen to the underlying immune cells. So, M-cells are antigen-presenting cells.\n\nB) Macrophages: These are immune cells that engulf and digest pathogens, dead cells, and debris. They also present antigens to T-cells to initiate an immune response. So, macrophages are antigen-presenting cells.\n\nC) Langerhans cells: These are specialized dendritic cells found in the skin and mucosal tissues. They capture and process antigens and present them to T-cells. So, Langerhans cells are antigen-presenting cells.\n\nD) Thymocytes: These are immature T-cells that develop in the thymus. They do not present antigens to other immune cells but instead undergo selection processes to become mature T-cells.\n\nSo, the correct answer is D) Thymocytes, as they are not antigen-presenting cells.",
