                    {
                        "id": "31855f11-096e-4dcf-9937-a4045647762f",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-09",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nEpistaxis, or nosebleed, is a common condition that can be caused by various factors such as trauma, inflammation, or vascular abnormalities. In severe cases, when conservative measures such as pressure, ice, or nasal packing fail to control bleeding, surgical intervention may be necessary. One of the most common surgical procedures for epistaxis control is arterial ligation, which involves the surgical division or occlusion of the bleeding vessel to stop the blood flow.\n\nIn the context of epistaxis control, the vessels that are commonly ligated include the sphenopalatine artery, the anterior and posterior ethmoidal arteries, and the internal maxillary artery. These vessels are located in the nasal cavity and supply blood to the nasal mucosa and the surrounding structures.\n\nNow, let's look at the options given in the question:\n\nA) Maxillary artery: This vessel is commonly ligated in cases of severe epistaxis, especially when the bleeding originates from the posterior part of the nasal cavity. The maxillary artery is a branch of the external carotid artery and supplies blood to the maxillary sinus, the palate, and the nasal cavity.\n\nB) Anterior ethmoidal artery: This vessel is also commonly ligated in cases of epistaxis, especially when the bleeding originates from the anterior part of the nasal cavity. The anterior ethmoidal artery is a branch of the ophthalmic artery and supplies blood to the ethmoidal air cells and the nasal septum.\n\nC) Internal carotid artery: This vessel is not ligated in cases of epistaxis control, as it is a major artery that supplies blood to the brain and other vital structures. Ligation of the internal carotid artery can lead to severe neurological complications and is only performed in rare cases of life-threatening bleeding.\n\nD) External carotid artery: This vessel is the main source of blood supply to the face, neck, and scalp, and has several branches that supply blood to the nasal cavity. The external carotid artery is commonly ligated in cases of severe epistaxis, especially when the bleeding originates from the anterior or middle part of the nasal cavity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer to the question is C) Internal carotid artery.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "f741fb6e-fbfe-4bcd-b3c0-f1200679958b",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "C) Internal carotid artery.",
                                "correct_answer": true
