                    {
                        "id": "cbae1ac3-61b8-4b90-9aa7-182b677e2e35",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nFirst, we need to determine the research question: How does the fat content in the expressed breast milk of pre-term infants compare to that of term infants?\n\nNext, we need to consider the study design that would be best suited to answer this question. \n\nA) Case control: This study design is typically used to investigate the association between an exposure and an outcome. It involves selecting cases (individuals with the outcome of interest) and controls (individuals without the outcome of interest) and comparing their exposure histories. This design would not be appropriate for our research question as we are not investigating an exposure, but rather comparing a characteristic (fat content in breast milk) between two groups of infants.\n\nB) Prospective cohort: This study design involves following a group of individuals over time and collecting data on their exposure(s) and outcome(s). This design could be used to compare the fat content in breast milk of pre-term and term infants, but it may be difficult to recruit and retain a cohort of pre-term infants due to their increased risk of health complications.\n\nC) Longitudinal study: This study design involves collecting data from the same group of individuals over an extended period of time. This design could be used to compare the fat content in breast milk of pre-term and term infants, and would allow for repeated measurements of breast milk fat content over time.\n\nD) Ambispective: This term is not commonly used in research design, but it typically refers to a study that combines retrospective and prospective data collection. This design may not be appropriate for our research question as we are interested in comparing the fat content in breast milk of pre-term and term infants at a specific point in time.\n\nBased on these considerations, the best study design for our research question would be a longitudinal study.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "0871d330-f215-460a-bbac-89565a9242c2",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "C) Longitudinal study.",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
                        ],
