                    {
                        "id": "9ee6115f-2613-4395-9183-71a4e31988a5",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-09",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe study is comparing the fat content in breast milk of pre-term and term infants. This suggests that the study will involve measuring the fat content in breast milk samples from both groups of infants. Therefore, a longitudinal study design would be best suited for this study. This design involves following a group of individuals over a period of time and collecting data at multiple time points. In this case, the study would involve collecting breast milk samples from both pre-term and term infants at different time points to compare the fat content. \n\nA case control study design involves comparing individuals with a particular condition (cases) to individuals without the condition (controls). This design would not be appropriate for this study as it does not involve comparing individuals with a specific condition. \n\nA prospective cohort study design involves following a group of individuals over time and collecting data on their exposure to a particular factor and their outcomes. This design would not be appropriate for this study as it does not involve measuring exposure to a particular factor. \n\nAn ambispective study design involves collecting data retrospectively and prospectively. This design would not be appropriate for this study as it involves collecting data from the past, which may not be feasible for measuring breast milk samples. \n\nTherefore, the best study design for this study is a longitudinal study.",
                        "answers": [
