                        "cot": "\n\nThe APGAR score is a quick assessment tool used to evaluate the physical condition of a newborn immediately after birth. It was developed by anesthesiologist Virginia Apgar in 1952.\n\nThe acronym APGAR stands for:\n\nD) Appearance, pulse, grimace, activity, respiration\n\nThe five components of the APGAR score are:\n\n1. Appearance (skin color)\n2. Pulse (heart rate)\n3. Grimace (reflex irritability)\n4. Activity (muscle tone)\n5. Respiration (breathing effort)\n\nEach component is scored on a scale of 0 to 2, with a maximum total score of 10. A score of 7 or above is considered normal, while a score of 3 or below indicates the need for immediate medical attention.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "ac688cd5-669d-4dcc-8bd0-622a17044fa9",
