                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nPhloem is a type of tissue in plants that transports sugars, amino acids, and other organic compounds from the leaves to other parts of the plant, such as the roots and flowers. This process is called translocation. \n\nA) Blood moves in a body: This is a good analogy because blood also transports nutrients and other substances throughout the body. However, phloem only transports organic compounds, while blood transports a wider range of substances, including oxygen, hormones, and waste products. \n\nB) Leaves move in the wind: This analogy doesn't make sense because leaves don't transport anything, they are simply moved by external forces. \n\nC) Water moves in a system: This is a decent analogy because water also moves through a system of pipes or channels, but water doesn't transport organic compounds like phloem does. \n\nD) Cars move on a street: This analogy doesn't make sense because cars are not living organisms and don't transport anything in the same way that phloem does. \n\nTherefore, the best answer is A) blood moves in a body.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "7c66e83d-e2d6-4720-9f10-9fd97f369009",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
