                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: The lunar cycle is approximately 29.5 days long.\n\nStep 2: A full moon occurs when the moon is on the opposite side of the Earth from the sun, so that its entire illuminated side is facing us.\n\nStep 3: Since the lunar cycle is 29.5 days long, there are approximately 12.37 lunar cycles in a year (365.25 days divided by 29.5 days).\n\nStep 4: Since a full moon occurs once during each lunar cycle, there are approximately 12.37 full moons in a year.\n\nStep 5: Rounding to the nearest whole number, we can say that there are generally 12 full moons in a year.\n\nStep 6: Dividing 12 by 12 months in a year, we get an average of one full moon per month.\n\nTherefore, the answer is C) once.",
